\fs28\b Smart School-Zone Traffic Controller Using a Verilog FSM\b0\par
\par
\b Problem Statement\b0\par
Traditional traffic-light controllers use fixed timing regardless of school hours or pedestrian activity. In school zones, this can create safety risks because children frequently cross the road. This project extends the Lab 10 traffic-light controller into a smart school-zone intersection controller using a Verilog finite state machine.\par
\par
\b Objectives\b0\par
1. Control traffic on the main road and side road.\par
2. Support normal mode and school mode.\par
3. Respond to pedestrian crossing requests.\par
4. Provide a walk signal with countdown timing.\par
5. Operate safely using proper state transitions.\par
6. Return to a safe state when reset is activated.\par
\par
\b System Inputs and Outputs\b0\par
Inputs: clk, reset, school_mode, flash_mode, ped_request.\par
Outputs: main_light, side_light, walk_light, countdown.\par
\par
\b FSM States\b0\par
S0 - Main Green\par
S1 - Main Yellow\par
S2 - Side Green\par
S3 - Side Yellow\par
S4 - Walk State / Pedestrian Crossing\par
\par
\b Required State Order\b0\par
Reset -> S0 Main Green -> S1 Main Yellow -> S2 Side Green -> S4 Walk State if ped_request is active -> S3 Side Yellow -> S0 Main Green.\par
\par
If no pedestrian request is active, the controller skips S4 and goes directly from S2 Side Green to S3 Side Yellow.\par
\par
\b State Table\b0\par
S0 Main Green: main_light = Green, side_light = Red, walk_light = Off.\par
S1 Main Yellow: main_light = Yellow, side_light = Red, walk_light = Off.\par
S2 Side Green: main_light = Red, side_light = Green, walk_light = Off.\par
S4 Walk State: main_light = Red, side_light = Red, walk_light = On, countdown active.\par
S3 Side Yellow: main_light = Red, side_light = Yellow, walk_light = Off.\par
\par
\b Timing Explanation\b0\par
In normal mode, main green lasts 10 seconds, main yellow lasts 3 seconds, side green lasts 8 seconds, and side yellow lasts 3 seconds.\par
\par
In school mode, main green is shortened to 8 seconds, side green is extended to 10 seconds, and the pedestrian walk countdown lasts 8 seconds.\par
\par
The simulation treats each clock tick as one timing unit. The countdown output displays the remaining pedestrian crossing time during the walk state.\par
\par
\b Pedestrian Request Behavior\b0\par
When ped_request is activated, the request is latched by the controller. It does not interrupt a yellow transition or immediately stop the current traffic phase. Instead, the controller finishes the safe sequence and enters the walk state after side green. During the walk state, both roads are red and walk_light is turned on.\par
\par
\b Flashing Yellow Mode\b0\par
The flash_mode input simulates inactive school periods outside school hours. When flash_mode = 1 and school_mode = 0, the main road flashes yellow while the side road remains red. This indicates caution while reducing unnecessary traffic delay.\par
\par
\b Safe Reset Behavior\b0\par
When reset = 1, the controller immediately returns to S0 Main Green. The outputs become main_light = Green, side_light = Red, walk_light = Off, and countdown = 0.\par
\par
\b Safety Rules\b0\par
1. Main and side roads are never green at the same time.\par
2. Yellow always appears before changing right-of-way.\par
3. Reset always returns to a safe starting state.\par
4. Walk signal is active only during the pedestrian walk state.\par
5. Pedestrian requests do not interrupt yellow transitions.\par
\par
\b Testbench Scenarios\b0\par
The testbench verifies reset operation, normal traffic cycle, school mode timing, flashing yellow mode, pedestrian request handling, walk countdown, reset during side green, multiple pedestrian requests while one is already being served, and the safety condition that both roads are never green simultaneously.\par
